            <ArticleTitle>Primary gastric tuberculosis mimicking gastric cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>177-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5152/UCD.2014.2667</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 42-year-old female patient with no previous known diseases who had complaints of postprandial epigastric pain and weight loss and who could not be diagnosed by endoscopic biopsy, although gastric cancer was suspected radiologically and endoscopically, was diagnosed with primary gastric tuberculosis by laparotomy and frozen section. Following anti-tuberculosis treatment, a complete clinical, radiological, and endoscopic response was achieved.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
